<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beaumaris Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1295</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beaumaris Castle is a castle on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a> in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, begun by Edward I in 1295 after a Welsh revolt and designed by Master James of St George. Built to a symmetrical concentric plan with a moat and dock, it is regarded as a masterpiece of medieval military architecture, though it was never finished. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caernarfon">Caernarfon</a>, Conwy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a> it forms a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beaumaris-castle.jpg" alt="Beaumaris Castle" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Llywelyn2000 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castell_Beaumaris_Castle,_Ynys_Mon_(Anglesey),_Wales_45.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
